4.1 What is Abstraction?
Definition: Abstraction is the process of hiding implementation details
and showing only the essential functionality to the user.


Real-life example
Think about driving a car.

When you drive a car, you use:
- The steering wheel to control direction.
- The accelerator to increase speed.
- The brake to slow down or stop.
You do not need to understand every internal detail of the engine to drive
the car. The car provides simple controls while hiding much of its internal
implementation. This illustrates abstraction.


class Car {

    void start() {
        System.out.println("Car started");
    }

    void drive() {
        System.out.println("Car is moving");
    }
}

public class Main {

    public static void main(String[] args) {

        Car c = new Car();

        c.start();
        c.drive();
    }
}


Output
Car started
Car is moving


The user calls start() and drive() without needing to know how those
operations are implemented internally.

This example illustrates the basic idea of abstraction, although Java's
formal abstraction mechanisms are primarily abstract classes and interfaces.


Java's formal abstraction mechanisms are primarily abstract classes and
interfaces.



4.2 The abstract Keyword
The abstract keyword is used to declare an abstract class or an abstract method.
- An abstract class cannot be instantiated directly.
- An abstract method has a declaration but no method body in the class
  where it is declared.

Example:
abstract class Animal {

    abstract void sound();

    void sleep() {
        System.out.println("Animal is sleeping");
    }
}


Here:
- abstract class Animal declares an abstract class.
- abstract void sound(); declares an abstract method.
- sleep() is a concrete method because it has a body.

You cannot create an object directly like this:

// Animal a = new Animal(); // Compilation error

But you can create a concrete subclass that implements the abstract method.







4.3 Abstract Classes
`Definition`
An abstract class is a class declared with the abstract keyword. It can
contain abstract methods, concrete methods, instance variables,
constructors, and static methods.

It is useful when related classes share common data or behavior but some
operations must be implemented differently by each subclass.


abstract class Animal {
    String name;

    Animal(String name) {
        this.name = name;
    }

    abstract void sound();

    void displayName() {
        System.out.println("Name: " + name);
    }

}

class Dog extends Animal {

    Dog(String name) {
        super(name);
    }

    @Override
    void sound() {
        System.out.println("Dog barks");
    }
}

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Dog d = new Dog("Tommy");
        d.displayName();
        d.sound();
    }
}



Explanation
- Animal is an abstract class.
- Animal(String name) is its constructor.
- displayName() is a concrete method inherited by Dog.
- sound() is an abstract method.
- Dog provides the implementation of sound().
- super(name) calls the parent constructor.

Important: An abstract class can have a constructor.
That constructor runs when a concrete subclass object is created,
even though you cannot instantiate the abstract class directly.

Rules of abstract classes
1. An abstract class cannot be instantiated directly.
2. It can contain both abstract and concrete methods.
3. It can contain instance variables and constructors.
4. A concrete subclass must implement all inherited abstract methods unless
   it is also abstract.
5. An abstract class can extend another class and implement interfaces.
6. An abstract class can exist without any abstract methods, if declared
   abstract.



4.4 Abstract Methods

`Definition`
An abstract method is a method declared without an implementation body.
Its declaration ends with a semicolon.

Syntax:
abstract returnType methodName(parameters);


Example:
abstract class Shape {
    abstract double calculateArea();
}


The class says that every concrete shape must provide a way to calculate
its area, but it does not define the formula.

Implementing an abstract method

abstract class Shape {
    abstract double calculateArea();
}

class Circle extends Shape {

    double radius;
    Circle(double radius) {
        this.radius = radius;
    }
    @Override
    double calculateArea() {
        return Math.PI * radius * radius;
    }
}

class Rectangle extends Shape {

    double length;
    double width;

    Rectangle(double length, double width) {
        this.length = length;
        this.width = width;
    }

    @Override
    double calculateArea() {
        return length * width;
    }
}

public class Main {

    public static void main(String[] args) {

        Shape s1 = new Circle(5);
        Shape s2 = new Rectangle(4, 6);

        System.out.println(s1.calculateArea());
        System.out.println(s2.calculateArea());
    }
}


Output
78.53981633974483
24.0


Why is this abstraction?
The Shape class defines what every shape should do: calculate its area.
Each subclass defines how to calculate it.
The same method call, calculateArea(), works for different shapes. This example also demonstrates runtime polymorphism.

Important rules
- An abstract method cannot have a method body in its declaration.
- A normal, concrete class cannot declare an abstract method.
- An abstract method cannot be private, static, or final, because it must be implementable by a subclass.
- A concrete subclass must implement inherited abstract methods.
- Abstract methods are implicitly overridable instance methods.



 you can write calculateArea() without the abstract keyword.

You use abstract not because Java cannot implement the methods without it,
but because you want Java to enforce a common rule and provide a shared
parent type.

If you have only one or two independent classes and no such requirement,
you may not need an abstract class at all.

Remember: abstract is useful when you want to say, "Every concrete child
class must provide this behavior, but the parent class cannot define one
suitable implementation for all children."






4.5 Interfaces
`Definition`

An interface in Java defines a contract that implementing classes agree to
follow.

An interface can declare abstract methods, constants, default methods,
static methods, and—since Java 9—private helper methods.

Interfaces are especially useful when classes may be unrelated by
inheritance but should support the same operation.

Example
interface Payment {
    void pay(double amount);
}

class UpiPayment implements Payment {
    @Override
    public void pay(double amount) {
        System.out.println("Paid ₹" + amount + " using UPI");
    }
}

class CardPayment implements Payment {

    @Override
    public void pay(double amount) {
        System.out.println("Paid ₹" + amount + " using Card");
    }
}

public class Main {

    public static void main(String[] args) {

        Payment p1 = new UpiPayment();
        Payment p2 = new CardPayment();

        p1.pay(500);
        p2.pay(1000);
    }
}


Output
Paid ₹500.0 using UPI
Paid ₹1000.0 using Card


Explanation
- Payment defines the contract: It says that every payment class must have
  a pay() method.
- pay() is implicitly public abstract: We don't need to write public
  abstract because Java adds them automatically.
- UpiPayment and CardPayment implement the interface: Both classes agree to
  provide their own pay() method.
- Each class provides its own implementation: UPI prints a UPI payment
  message, while Card prints a card payment message.
- The Payment reference can refer to either implementation: We can use the
  same reference type, Payment, to work with both UPI and Card payment objects.
- Why is public required? Interface methods are public, so the implementing
  method must also be public. We cannot reduce its visibility.

Easy way to remember
Interface = Rules to follow.
Payment says what a payment class must do. UpiPayment and CardPayment decide how to do it.


4.6 The implements Keyword
Definition
The implements keyword is used by a class to declare that it implements an
interface.

Syntax:
class ClassName implements InterfaceName {
    // Implement interface methods
}



extends vs implements

extends	                             implements
A class extends another class.	A class implements an interface.
A class can extend only 	    A class can implement multiple interfaces.
one direct superclass.
Used for class inheritance.  	Used to fulfill interface contracts.
Example: class Dog extends Animal	Example: class Dog implements Runnable

A class can also extend one class and implement multiple interfaces:
class SmartPhone extends Phone
        implements Camera, MusicPlayer {
}


This is valid if Phone is a class and Camera and MusicPlayer are interfaces. A concrete SmartPhone class must implement their required abstract methods.





# Multiple Inheritance Through Interfaces in Java

## 1. What Is Multiple Inheritance?

**Multiple inheritance means a class inherits from more than one parent class.**

For example, conceptually:


class C extends A, B {
}


Java does not allow a class to extend multiple classes.
However, Java allows a class to implement multiple interfaces.


class C implements A, B {
}


## 2. Why Does Java Not Support Multiple Class Inheritance?

Suppose two parent classes have a method with the same name.


class A {

    void sound() {
        System.out.println("Sound from A");
    }
}

class B {

    void sound() {
        System.out.println("Sound from B");
    }
}


Now imagine Java allowed:

```java
class C extends A, B {
}
```

If we create an object and call:

```java
C obj = new C();
obj.sound();
```

Which method should execute?

- `A.sound()` → prints `Sound from A`
- `B.sound()` → prints `Sound from B`

This creates ambiguity because both parent classes provide their own
implementation of `sound()`.

**Therefore, Java does not allow a class to extend multiple classes.**



## 3. How Do Interfaces Help?

An interface can declare a method without providing its implementation.

```java
interface A {

    void sound();
}

interface B {

    void sound();
}
```

Both interfaces declare `sound()`, but neither provides a method body.

Now a class can implement both interfaces:

```java
class C implements A, B {

    @Override
    public void sound() {
        System.out.println("Sound from C");
    }
}
```

Using the class:

```java
class Main {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        C obj = new C();
        obj.sound();
    }
}
```

Output:

```text
Sound from C
```


### Why Is There No Confusion?

Both interfaces require the `sound()` method, but neither supplies a
competing implementation.

Class `C` provides one implementation that satisfies both interfaces.

```text
Interface A → requires sound()
                     \
                      → Class C implements sound()
                     /
Interface B → requires sound()
```


## 4. What If Both Interfaces Have Default Methods?

Interfaces can also provide method implementations using `default` methods.

```java
interface A {

    default void sound() {
        System.out.println("Sound from A");
    }
}

interface B {

    default void sound() {
        System.out.println("Sound from B");
    }
}
```

If a class implements both interfaces, Java cannot choose between these
conflicting default implementations automatically.

The class must override the method to resolve the conflict.

```java
class C implements A, B {

    @Override
    public void sound() {
        System.out.println("Sound from C");
    }
}
```

Output:

```text
Sound from C
```

## 5. Important Interview Points

1. Java does not support extending multiple classes.
2. Java allows a class to implement multiple interfaces.
3. Interfaces can declare methods that implementing classes must implement.
4. One implementation can satisfy the same abstract method declared by multiple interfaces.
5. If two interfaces provide conflicting default methods, the implementing class must resolve the conflict.
6. This feature is commonly called **multiple inheritance through interfaces**.

## 6. One-Line Revision

**Java avoids ambiguity from multiple class inheritance by allowing a class
 to implement multiple interfaces. When conflicting default implementations
 exist, the class must resolve the conflict.**






4.8 Default and Static Methods in Interfaces

An interface can contain methods with implementations. Two important types
are default methods and static methods.

A. Default Methods
Definition: A default method is a method inside an interface that already
has a method body.

We use the default keyword to declare it.
Example
interface Vehicle {

    default void start() {
        System.out.println("Vehicle is starting");
    }
}

class Car implements Vehicle {
}

class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {

        Car c = new Car();
        c.start();
    }
}


Output:
Vehicle is starting



- Vehicle is an interface.
- start() is a default method with an implementation.
- Car implements Vehicle.
- Car does not need to write its own start() method because the interface
  already provides one.
- When we call c.start(), the default implementation executes.

Can a class change the default method?
Yes. A class can override the default method to provide its own implementation.
class Car implements Vehicle {

    @Override
    public void start() {
        System.out.println("Car engine started");
    }
}


Now c.start() prints:
Car engine started


Remember: A default method provides a ready-made implementation that an
          implementing class can use or override.



B. Static Methods in Interfaces

Definition: A static method belongs to the interface itself, not to an
implementing object's instance.

We declare it using the static keyword and call it using the interface name.

Example
interface Calculator {

    static int add(int a, int b) {
        return a + b;
    }
}

class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {

        System.out.println(Calculator.add(10, 20));
    }
}


Output:
30

Explanation
- Calculator is an interface.
- add() is a static method with an implementation.
- It adds two numbers and returns the result.
- We call it using Calculator.add(10, 20).
- We don't need to create a Calculator object.

Remember: A static method belongs to the interface, so call it using the interface name.



C. Default Method vs Static Method
Default method	          Static method
Declared using default	   Declared using static
Has a method body	        Has a method body
Called using an implementing object 	Called using the interface name
Can be overridden by an implementing class	Cannot be overridden as an instance method
Example: c.start()	Example: Calculator.add(10, 20)


