4.9 Abstract Class vs Interface

This is one of the most important Java interview questions.
Both abstract classes and interfaces support abstraction, but they serve
different purposes.

Example: Abstract class
Use an abstract class when related classes share common state and
implementation.

abstract class Employee {

    String name;

    Employee(String name) {
        this.name = name;
    }

    void displayName() {
        System.out.println(name);
    }

    abstract double calculateSalary();
}

class FullTimeEmployee extends Employee {

    FullTimeEmployee(String name) {
        super(name);
    }

    @Override
    double calculateSalary() {
        return 50000;
    }
}


The abstract class stores shared employee data and provides common behavior.

Example: Interface
Use an interface when you want to define a capability that different
classes can implement.

interface Payable {
    void makePayment();
}

class EmployeePayment implements Payable {
    @Override
    public void makePayment() {
        System.out.println("Employee payment processed");
    }
}

class CustomerPayment implements Payable {
    @Override
    public void makePayment() {
        System.out.println("Customer payment processed");
    }
}


Both classes can perform a payment, even though they represent different
kinds of entities.


Complete comparison table

Feature	            Abstract class	               Interface
Declaration	          abstract class	           interface

Object creation	 Cannot instantiate directly	Cannot instantiate directly

Methods	         Abstract and concrete methods	Abstract, default, static,
                                                and private methods

Instance variables	Allowed	Not allowed;        fields are constants

Constructors	      Allowed	                  Not allowed

State	Can maintain instance state	         Cannot maintain per-object
                                              instance fields

Inheritance	A class can extend one class	A class can implement multiple interfaces

Keywords	    extends                     	implements

Main purpose	Shared state and implementation	Contract or capability

Example	           Employee	                    Payable


When should you use each?
Use an abstract class when:
- Several related classes share common fields.
- They need a common constructor.
- You want to provide shared implementation.
- You want subclasses to implement some specific methods.

Use an interface when:
- Different classes should follow the same contract.
- You want a class to support multiple capabilities.
- You want to separate the required behavior from a particular class hierarchy.

Interview answer: An abstract class is useful for sharing state and
implementation among related classes, whereas an interface defines a
contract that classes can implement, including classes from different
inheritance hierarchies.


One example that makes the difference clearer
Imagine you have:
- Circle
- Rectangle
- Triangle

All three are shapes. They share common state, such as color, and common
behavior, such as showColor(). An abstract class is a natural place to put
that shared implementation.

Now imagine Circle, Printer, and PaymentService all need a start() operation.
They aren't all the same kind of object, but they can all support that capability.
An interface is useful for defining that shared contract.

Remember this
You're correct that both can require subclasses to implement methods.
That is not the main difference.
- Use an abstract class when you want a shared base with common state,
  constructors, or implementation.
- Use an interface when you want to define a capability or contract that
  potentially unrelated classes can implement.



4.10 Real-World Abstraction Examples
Here are three examples that connect abstraction to practical software development.




Example 1: ATM system


What the user sees
- Withdraw money
- Check balance
- Deposit money

What is hidden
- Transaction validation
- Communication with banking systems
- Account verification
- Transaction processing

A user interacts with simple operations without needing to know every
internal implementation detail.

Java illustration:
abstract class ATM {
    abstract void withdraw(double amount);
    abstract void checkBalance();
}


Different ATM implementations can provide the actual transaction logic.


Example 3: Notification system
An application may send notifications through email or SMS.
interface Notification {

    void send(String message);
}

class EmailNotification implements Notification {

    @Override
    public void send(String message) {
        System.out.println("Sending email: " + message);
    }
}

class SMSNotification implements Notification {

    @Override
    public void send(String message) {
        System.out.println("Sending SMS: " + message);
    }
}


Usage:
public class Main {

    public static void main(String[] args) {

        Notification n = new EmailNotification();
        n.send("Welcome!");

        n = new SMSNotification();
        n.send("Your OTP is 1234");
    }
}


Output:
Sending email: Welcome!
Sending SMS: Your OTP is 1234


This example demonstrates both abstraction and runtime polymorphism: the Notification interface defines the contract, and the actual implementation determines how the message is sent.







